From: j7
Subject: j7: reach the top CPU step through OSCCLK, not the MIF switch

The 0395 image did not reach the logo and left no new log, which is what a stopped CPU
clock looks like: the raise runs in AppleCLCD::start, before the logo and before the
kernel's log flush, so a core that stops there writes nothing anywhere.

0395 switched the cores to the MIF switch before touching the PLL, but the mux status it
polled (+0x608 bits 12:13) reports which input the mux selected, not that the input has a
clock.  The 0380 boot is the proof of a transition that does work: it never wrote +0x204,
so the switch input was OSCCLK at 26 MHz, and the machine stayed alive - it only reported
cpu_mhz=38 because it waited for status 1 instead of 2 and returned before programming the
PLL.  This patch keeps that OSCCLK detour and drops every CMU_MIF write.

Per cluster: ARMCLK onto the switch input (+0x208 bit 12) with +0x204 left alone, poll
+0x608 for 2, write the lock time and PLL_CON0 (M 276 / P 4 / S 0 is 1794 MHz), poll lock
29, move ARMCLK back and poll 1.  The polls use IODelay rather than IOSleep, because at
26 MHz a scheduling sleep is a very long time.  A mux that does not move is put back, and a
PLL that never locks gets its old CON0 back with the cores returned to it.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
@@ -244,13 +244,9 @@
  *   CMU_CPUCL0 0x10900000, CMU_CPUCL1 0x10800000
  *     +0x000 PLL lock time, written P * 150
  *     +0x100 PLL_CON0, M 16:25, P 8:13, S 0:2, bit 5 set, bit 26 clear, enable 31, locked 29
- *     +0x204 the switch source mux: bit 12 = 1 selects the MIF switch, bit 27 = 0 takes that
- *             selection out of hardware control
+ *     +0x204 the switch source mux (left at the bootloader's value: its 0 input is OSCCLK)
  *     +0x208 the ARMCLK mux, bit 12: 0 = the cluster PLL, 1 = the switch input
  *     +0x608 its status, bits 12:13, which reads 1 << the value written (2 for the switch)
- *   CMU_MIF 0x10460000
- *     +0x858/+0x85c the switch gate for cluster 0/1, bit 0
- *     +0x458/+0x45c the switch divider, bits 0:1; 0 is the switch table's 800 MHz step
  *
  * 0380 died at 38 MHz because of two mistakes.  It never selected the MIF switch on the source
  * mux at +0x204, so moving the cores "onto the switch" moved them onto OSCCLK, 26 MHz.  And it
@@ -259,12 +255,20 @@
  * really were on the oscillator branch.  The PLL was never programmed, and every store and every
  * timing loop then ran at standby clock.
  *
- * The sequence is the vendor DFS one: enable the switch gate and select it on the source mux,
- * set the switch divider to its 800 MHz step, move ARMCLK onto the switch and wait for the
- * status, program the PLL and its lock time and poll for lock, move ARMCLK back and wait, then
- * put the switch source back the way it was.  A cluster whose switch never reports is left
- * untouched; a PLL that never locks gets the old value back and the cores are returned to it,
- * so the worst case is the 902 MHz operating point this boot started from.
+ * The transition runs the cores from OSCCLK, not from the MIF switch.  That is the one
+ * transition this phone has already survived: 0380 moved ARMCLK to the switch input without
+ * touching +0x204, so the input was OSCCLK, and the machine kept running (cpu_mhz=38) - it only
+ * failed because it waited for status 1 where the switch reads 2 and returned before the PLL was
+ * programmed.  0395 selected the MIF switch and wrote CMU_MIF +0x858/+0x458 first; the mux status
+ * only says the mux selected that input, not that the switch clock is running, and that boot
+ * hung before the logo and before any log flush.  OSCCLK is always running, so it is the safe
+ * stand-in for the transition, and no CMU_MIF register is written at all.
+ *
+ * Per cluster: ARMCLK to the switch input (+0x208 bit 12), poll +0x608 for 2, write the PLL lock
+ * time and CON0, poll lock 29, move back and poll 1.  The polls use IODelay, not IOSleep: at
+ * 26 MHz a scheduling sleep is an eternity.  A cluster whose mux does not move is left alone, and
+ * a PLL that never locks gets its old CON0 back with the cores returned to it, so the worst case
+ * is the 902 MHz operating point this boot started from.
  */
 static bool
 j7_cpu_pll(unsigned khz, uint32_t *m_out)
@@ -283,13 +287,9 @@
 static void
 j7_cpu_clock(unsigned khz)
 {
-	static const struct {
-		uint64_t cmu;
-		const char *name;
-		uint32_t gate, swdiv;
-	} cpu[] = {
-		{ 0x10900000ULL, "CPUCL0", 0x858u, 0x458u },
-		{ 0x10800000ULL, "CPUCL1", 0x85cu, 0x45cu },
+	static const struct { uint64_t cmu; const char *name; } cpu[] = {
+		{ 0x10900000ULL, "CPUCL0" },
+		{ 0x10800000ULL, "CPUCL1" },
 	};
 	uint32_t m;
 
@@ -297,15 +297,6 @@
 		IOLog("A5XELTE CPU: %u kHz is not a step this PLL makes\n", khz);
 		return;
 	}
-	IOMemoryDescriptor *mifDesc = IOMemoryDescriptor::withPhysicalAddress(
-	    0x10460000ULL, 0x1000, kIODirectionNone);
-	IOMemoryMap *mifMap = mifDesc != NULL ? mifDesc->map(kIOMapInhibitCache) : NULL;
-	if (mifMap == NULL) {
-		IOLog("A5XELTE CPU: no CMU_MIF map\n");
-		OSSafeReleaseNULL(mifDesc);
-		return;
-	}
-	volatile uint32_t *mif = (volatile uint32_t *)mifMap->getVirtualAddress();
 	for (unsigned i = 0; i < sizeof(cpu) / sizeof(cpu[0]); i++) {
 		IOMemoryDescriptor *desc = IOMemoryDescriptor::withPhysicalAddress(
 		    cpu[i].cmu, 0x1000, kIODirectionNone);
@@ -321,10 +312,9 @@
 		const uint64_t pll = (cp != 0 && cs < 7) ? 26000ULL * cm / ((uint64_t)cp << cs) : 0;
 		const uint32_t d1 = c[0x400 / 4] & 7u, d2 = c[0x404 / 4] & 7u;
